# Agent Experience (core.AgentExperience)

## Definition
The design category for agent-facing interfaces, artifacts, protocols, and feedback loops across both structured machine consumers and token-sensitive harnessed LLMs. AX includes structured projections such as JSON for parsers and compact projections for LLM/harness contexts; durable truth should remain in authoritative artifacts rather than token-heavy prose.

## Examples
- A JSON run artifact or report projection consumed by deterministic parsers, CI systems, dashboards, or programmatic agents.
- A compact line-oriented projection such as --compact or doctor --machine for harnessed LLMs where token budget and next-action clarity matter.
- An actionable evidence-closure packet that gives an agent the target files, commands, evidence basis, and completion criteria for the next safe step.

## Anti-examples
- Human-first visual interface polish by itself; that is UX unless it is specifically shaping an agent-facing surface.
- Developer ergonomics for library authors by itself; that is DX unless it defines agent-facing artifacts or projections.
- Raw verbose JSON dumped into an LLM context when a compact projection would preserve the same action semantics with fewer tokens.

## Notes
- Rationale: UX and DX already name human and developer experience. ai-society needs a shared term for agent-facing usability without collapsing structured machine contracts and compact harnessed-LLM projections into one format.
- Use UX for human-facing experience and DX for developer-facing experience.
- Use AX as the umbrella; --json and --compact can both be AX projections when they serve agent consumers.
- Prefer --json for structured machine consumers and --compact for token-sensitive harnessed LLMs.
